{
  "dom_id": "step-q103806",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi )$ for which the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ has vertical tangents, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\theta  & =\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi n}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=0,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is also a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has vertical tangents at $\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/b213cb7ed5d806d9fecb66a34306c93506ff9dc3719b5b49c4f5edad275e1e0a.png)",
